1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:05,970
Kerala's tourist numbers can't tell me how many families need a room near a hospital.

2
00:00:06,020 --> 00:00:08,050
So I started with something I could count:

3
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:09,193
hospital beds.

4
00:00:09,843 --> 00:00:13,093
Amrita Kochi lists about fourteen hundred and fifty beds.

5
00:00:13,143 --> 00:00:15,943
Aster Medcity, seven hundred inpatient beds.

6
00:00:15,993 --> 00:00:21,953
At Aster's reported Kerala occupancy, that's roughly half a million patient bed-days a year.

7
00:00:22,003 --> 00:00:24,073
Everything after that is an assumption.

8
00:00:24,723 --> 00:00:25,813
In the base case:

9
00:00:25,863 --> 00:00:31,313
about seventy thousand room-nights, or sixteen crore rupees a year around these two hospitals.

10
00:00:31,363 --> 00:00:36,343
Around half is self-arranged, mid-priced stays a platform could serve — about eight crore.

11
00:00:36,393 --> 00:00:39,398
A ten-property pilot might reach seven percent of that.

12
00:00:40,048 --> 00:00:42,058
These are projections, not data.

13
00:00:42,108 --> 00:00:49,838
The two inputs that matter most — how many families live too far to commute, and how many need a paid room — have no public source.

14
00:00:49,888 --> 00:00:53,326
Shift them, and the market ranges from five crore to thirty-five.

15
00:00:53,976 --> 00:00:55,906
I compared five revenue models.

16
00:00:55,956 --> 00:01:01,756
Commission earns the most at pilot scale — but a family staying three weeks can easily rebook direct.

17
00:01:01,806 --> 00:01:06,504
A hybrid, with a lower commission plus a verified-listing fee, earns about the same.

18
00:01:07,154 --> 00:01:08,244
The base pilot:

19
00:01:08,294 --> 00:01:12,594
ten properties, forty rooms, and eighteen percent of nights booked through The Veedu.

20
00:01:12,644 --> 00:01:17,143
That projects about fifty-eight lakh in bookings, and seven lakh in platform revenue.

21
00:01:17,843 --> 00:01:25,703
With lean fixed costs and unpaid founder time, break-even needs around fifty-five bookings a month, or sixteen properties.

22
00:01:25,753 --> 00:01:27,225
The base case falls short.

23
00:01:27,875 --> 00:01:31,035
The conservative case loses money on every booking.

24
00:01:31,085 --> 00:01:34,145
The optimistic case breaks even with six properties.

25
00:01:34,195 --> 00:01:38,014
And at a fifteen percent commission, the base case roughly breaks even.

26
00:01:38,664 --> 00:01:40,674
So, does The Veedu deserve a pilot?

27
00:01:40,724 --> 00:01:42,544
Yes — a small, founder-led one.

28
00:01:42,594 --> 00:01:46,334
The numbers aren't proven, but they're close enough to test cheaply.

29
00:01:46,384 --> 00:01:48,104
First, validate three things:

30
00:01:48,154 --> 00:01:56,811
how many out-of-town families need a paid room, what it costs to reach them through hospital desks, and whether they'll book through The Veedu instead of going direct.
